    "result_text": "I've now fully traced the rejection-handling path. Here's what I found:\n\n## The change is already satisfied by the existing code \u2014 verified green\n\nThe new test `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (added after the advice) passes, and so does the full suite:\n\n```\nTests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0\nBUILD SUCCESS\n```\n\n### Why every rejection already complies\n\nEvery 400/409/429 rejection in this app is raised as an exception and funneled through the single `@ControllerAdvice`, `ExceptionControllerAdvice`. I cross-checked each path:\n\n- **All custom validation exceptions have a handler** \u2014 `MissingOwnerFields`, `InvalidTelephone`, `InvalidEmail`, `DisposableEmailDomain`, `InvalidPostcode`, `FutureRegistrationDate` (400), `DuplicateHousehold`, `CityOwnerCapacityExceeded` (409), `DailyRegistrationLimitExceeded` (429).\n- **Bean Validation** on `@Valid @RequestBody` \u2192 `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (400) \u2014 also handled. No `@Validated` method-param constraints exist that could throw an unhandled `ConstraintViolationException`/`HandlerMethodValidationException`.\n- Controllers only ever emit `2xx`/`404` directly; no manual 400/409/429 bodies bypass the advice. There is exactly one `@ControllerAdvice` and no other `ErrorController`/`ResponseEntityExceptionHandler`.\n\nEvery handler builds its body via `detailBuild(...)`, which produces a Spring `ProblemDetail` populating all four required members:\n\n```java\nProblemDetail problemDetail = ProblemDetail.forStatus(status); // -> \"status\"\nproblemDetail.setType(URI.create(url.toString()));             // -> \"type\"\nproblemDetail.setTitle(e.getClass().getSimpleName());          // -> \"title\"\nproblemDetail.setDetail(detail);                               // -> \"detail\"\n```\n\nReturning `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` makes Spring's message conversion emit `Content-Type: application/problem+json` (confirmed by the test \u2014 `application/json` is *not* compatible with `application/problem+json`, so the assertion would fail otherwise), with the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\nI made no code changes because none were needed \u2014 the specification is met and the suite is green. \n\nOne optional, non-blocking refinement I noticed (not required by the spec, which only mandates the four members be *present*): RFC7807 semantics would put the request URL in `instance` and use a stable problem-type URI for `type`; here `type` carries the request URL. I left this as-is to avoid churn, but I can adjust it if you'd like stricter RFC7807 semantics.",
